From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: add MediaTek MT6685 RTC

The RTC of the MT6897 boards is in the MT6685 on the SPMI bus. Without it
the time is wrong until the network is up.

This is based on the MediaTek driver, in its version in the OnePlus MT6897
kernel, and on the one AngeloGioacchino Del Regno posted (v1 2/2, see the
link below). It differs from that second one in these ways:

 - The year register counts from 1968, as in the MediaTek driver and in
   the stock kernel of the Lenovo TB375FC. The posted driver takes the
   register as years since 1900 and leaves a start date of 1968 to the RTC
   core, which only offsets the years outside of 1900-2027. So 2026, a
   register value of 58 from Android, reads as 2086 there, and writing
   2026 puts 126 in the register, which Android reads as 2094. A device
   can go on running Android next to this, so the register has to mean the
   same to both.

 - Reading doesn't write to BBPU. MediaTek has dropped the BBPU reload from
   the read path in 2025 ("rtc: Fix power-on timing and add delay" in the
   Motorola kernel, and the OnePlus one has that too), and only opens the
   clocks for a moment before reading. So the registers must follow the
   counter while they have their clocks, though nothing says so in words.
   The stock module of this tablet is older and still has the reload. BBPU
   also holds the power enable and the commands that clear the power key
   and the spare registers, so it is better left alone.

 - The two clocks are gated from here with the sequence of the MediaTek
   driver. The posted driver needs the SCK_TOP clock controller and its
   changes to the common MediaTek clock code for these two gates alone.

 - No alarm, for now. The interrupt needs more than this driver:
   spmi-mtk-pmif only clears the event of a SID from a chained handler, so
   requesting the interrupt of SID 9 directly would keep the parent
   interrupt firing, and that one also has the power key. So this waits
   until it can be tried on a device. Without an alarm, this writes nothing
   to the alarm registers, IRQ_EN, BBPU and SCK_TOP_INT. A power-off alarm
   that Android has left armed stays armed, and if it goes off under Linux
   nothing clears its event: spmi-mtk-pmif doesn't look at a SID without a
   handler. That is so with or without this driver.

The bootloader and Android keep the reason for a boot, the state of the
power key and a power-off alarm in the same registers. So this only ever
writes the two clock gates, six time registers and the write trigger, and
the regmap refuses anything else. The MediaTek driver, the bootloader and
the posted driver all write these registers as two bytes, so this does too,
with the same values: the high bytes are 0. It never reads IRQ_STA either,
which clears when read, and the read table keeps debugfs away from it. That
table also has the chip ID, the gates and the lock state, for debugfs to
tell what went wrong on a device. Probing itself writes nothing, though the
RTC core reads the time twice while registering, and each read opens and
closes the gates. The week day register is neither read nor written: the
MediaTek driver writes it from an unset variable.

The MediaTek driver also writes at shutdown: it clears the power enable in
BBPU and the alarm in IRQ_EN, sets them again for a power-off alarm, and
sets TOP2_ELR1 for a normal power off sequence. The bootloader sets
TOP2_ELR1 too. It is not known yet if this needs that to power off. That is
for the device.

The registers and bits have been compared with the MediaTek headers, the
stock module and the tables of the bootloader by a script. It has run on a
register model with a fake SPMI controller in QEMU, but not on a device. It
is not known if the clocks alone refresh the time on this tablet, though
newer kernels of the same SoC rely on it.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20251024083318.25890-3-angelogioacchino.delregno@collabora.com
---
 drivers/rtc/Kconfig      |  12 ++
 drivers/rtc/Makefile     |   1 +
 drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6685.c | 426 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 439 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/Kconfig b/drivers/rtc/Kconfig
index 01def82..27ec870 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/rtc/Kconfig
@@ -1956,6 +1956,18 @@ config RTC_DRV_MT6397
 
 	  If you want to use MediaTek(R) RTC interface, select Y or M here.
 
+config RTC_DRV_MT6685
+	tristate "MediaTek MT6685 Clock IC based RTC"
+	depends on MFD_MTK_SPMI_PMIC || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on SPMI
+	select REGMAP_SPMI
+	help
+	  Say yes here to enable support for the Real Time Clock found
+	  in the MediaTek MT6685 Clock IC communicating over SPMI.
+
+	  This driver can also be built as a module. If so, the module
+	  will be called rtc-mt6685.
+
 config RTC_DRV_MT7622
 	tristate "MediaTek SoC based RTC"
 	depends on ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST
diff --git a/drivers/rtc/Makefile b/drivers/rtc/Makefile
index 0347645..57f69cb 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/rtc/Makefile
@@ -115,6 +115,7 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_SSD202D)	+= rtc-ssd202d.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MSM6242)	+= rtc-msm6242.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MT2712)	+= rtc-mt2712.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MT6397)	+= rtc-mt6397.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MT6685)	+= rtc-mt6685.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MT7622)	+= rtc-mt7622.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MV)	+= rtc-mv.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_MXC)	+= rtc-mxc.o
diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6685.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6685.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..593e33d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-mt6685.c
@@ -0,0 +1,426 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * MediaTek MT6685 Clock IC - Real Time Clock IP driver
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2020 MediaTek Inc.
+ * Copyright (C) 2025 Collabora Ltd.
+ *
+ * Based on the MediaTek driver and on the one posted by
+ * AngeloGioacchino Del Regno <angelogioacchino.delregno@collabora.com>.
+ *
+ * The bootloader and the other operating system on a device keep what they need
+ * in this block too: the reason for a boot, the state of the power key, a
+ * power-off alarm. So this driver only reads and sets the time, and its regmap
+ * refuses to write anywhere else.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/array_size.h>
+#include <linux/bits.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/device.h>
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
+#include <linux/rtc.h>
+#include <linux/spmi.h>
+#include <linux/time.h>
+
+/* The chip ID, read only through debugfs */
+#define MT6685_HWCID			0x008
+
+/* The clock gates of the RTC registers in SCK_TOP. A set bit gates a clock. */
+#define MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN		0x514
+#define MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_SET	0x515
+#define MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_CLR	0x516
+#define  MT6685_RTC_MCLK_PDN		BIT(4)
+#define  MT6685_RTC_32K_PDN		BIT(5)
+
+/*
+ * The RTC of the normal world. The one of the secure world is at 0x600. These
+ * registers are 16 bits wide, in two bytes on the SPMI bus.
+ */
+#define MT6685_RTC_BBPU			0x588
+#define  MT6685_RTC_BBPU_CBUSY		BIT(6)
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC		0x592
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_MIN		0x594
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_HOU		0x596
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_DOM		0x598
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_MTH		0x59c
+#define MT6685_RTC_TC_YEA		0x59e
+#define MT6685_RTC_WRTGR		0x5c2
+/* Tells if the registers are locked, read only through debugfs */
+#define MT6685_RTC_SPAR_MACRO		0x5d8
+
+#define MT6685_RTC_CBUSY_POLL_US	10
+#define MT6685_RTC_CBUSY_TIMEOUT_US	USEC_PER_SEC
+
+/* What the registers need to follow the clocks, as in the MediaTek driver */
+#define MT6685_RTC_CLOCK_DELAY_US	1000
+#define MT6685_RTC_REFRESH_DELAY_US	60
+#define MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_DELAY_US	2000
+
+#define MT6685_RTC_READ_TRIES		3
+#define MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_TRIES		2
+
+/* A write that took may have ticked this many seconds when it is read back */
+#define MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_MAX_TICK	2
+
+/*
+ * The MediaTek kernels take a year register of 0 as 1968. The hardware has a
+ * leap year in every fourth year from 0, which holds until 2095.
+ */
+#define MT6685_RTC_YEAR_OFFSET		68
+
+enum mt6685_rtc_tc_field {
+	MT6685_TC_SEC,
+	MT6685_TC_MIN,
+	MT6685_TC_HOUR,
+	MT6685_TC_DOM,
+	MT6685_TC_MON,
+	MT6685_TC_YEAR,
+	MT6685_TC_COUNT,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The time is in the low bytes. The MediaTek driver writes the day of the week
+ * from an unset variable and never reads it, so it is not here.
+ */
+static const struct {
+	u16 reg;
+	u8 mask;
+} mt6685_rtc_tc_regs[MT6685_TC_COUNT] = {
+	[MT6685_TC_SEC] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC, GENMASK(5, 0) },
+	[MT6685_TC_MIN] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_MIN, GENMASK(5, 0) },
+	[MT6685_TC_HOUR] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_HOU, GENMASK(4, 0) },
+	[MT6685_TC_DOM] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_DOM, GENMASK(4, 0) },
+	[MT6685_TC_MON] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_MTH, GENMASK(3, 0) },
+	[MT6685_TC_YEAR] = { MT6685_RTC_TC_YEA, GENMASK(6, 0) },
+};
+
+/*
+ * The counter runs from its own clock. The registers show it only after they
+ * have had their clocks for a moment, and a write needs the clocks to take
+ * effect.
+ */
+static int mt6685_rtc_ungate(struct regmap *map)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_write(map, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_CLR, MT6685_RTC_32K_PDN);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_write(map, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_CLR, MT6685_RTC_MCLK_PDN);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	fsleep(MT6685_RTC_CLOCK_DELAY_US);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_gate(struct regmap *map)
+{
+	int ret, ret_mclk;
+
+	ret = regmap_write(map, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_SET, MT6685_RTC_32K_PDN);
+	ret_mclk = regmap_write(map, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_SET,
+				MT6685_RTC_MCLK_PDN);
+
+	fsleep(MT6685_RTC_CLOCK_DELAY_US);
+
+	return ret ?: ret_mclk;
+}
+
+/* Every driver of this chip writes both bytes, the low one first */
+static int mt6685_rtc_write_reg(struct regmap *map, unsigned int reg,
+				unsigned int val)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_write(map, reg, val);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return regmap_write(map, reg + 1, 0);
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_read_tc(struct regmap *map, struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	unsigned int val[MT6685_TC_COUNT];
+	int i, ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < MT6685_TC_COUNT; i++) {
+		ret = regmap_read(map, mt6685_rtc_tc_regs[i].reg, &val[i]);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		val[i] &= mt6685_rtc_tc_regs[i].mask;
+	}
+
+	tm->tm_sec = val[MT6685_TC_SEC];
+	tm->tm_min = val[MT6685_TC_MIN];
+	tm->tm_hour = val[MT6685_TC_HOUR];
+	tm->tm_mday = val[MT6685_TC_DOM];
+	tm->tm_mon = (int)val[MT6685_TC_MON] - 1;
+	tm->tm_year = val[MT6685_TC_YEAR] + MT6685_RTC_YEAR_OFFSET;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_write_tc(struct regmap *map, const struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	const unsigned int val[MT6685_TC_COUNT] = {
+		[MT6685_TC_SEC] = tm->tm_sec,
+		[MT6685_TC_MIN] = tm->tm_min,
+		[MT6685_TC_HOUR] = tm->tm_hour,
+		[MT6685_TC_DOM] = tm->tm_mday,
+		[MT6685_TC_MON] = tm->tm_mon + 1,
+		[MT6685_TC_YEAR] = tm->tm_year - MT6685_RTC_YEAR_OFFSET,
+	};
+	int i, ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < MT6685_TC_COUNT; i++) {
+		ret = mt6685_rtc_write_reg(map, mt6685_rtc_tc_regs[i].reg,
+					   val[i]);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_refresh(struct regmap *map)
+{
+	int ret, ret_gate;
+
+	ret = mt6685_rtc_ungate(map);
+	if (!ret)
+		fsleep(MT6685_RTC_REFRESH_DELAY_US);
+
+	ret_gate = mt6685_rtc_gate(map);
+
+	return ret ?: ret_gate;
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_read_time(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	struct regmap *map = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int tries = MT6685_RTC_READ_TRIES;
+	unsigned int sec;
+	int ret;
+
+	do {
+		if (!tries--)
+			return -EIO;
+
+		ret = mt6685_rtc_refresh(map);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = mt6685_rtc_read_tc(map, tm);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = regmap_read(map, MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC, &sec);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	} while ((sec & mt6685_rtc_tc_regs[MT6685_TC_SEC].mask) < tm->tm_sec);
+
+	ret = rtc_valid_tm(tm);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* This fills in the day of the week */
+	rtc_time64_to_tm(rtc_tm_to_time64(tm), tm);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* If the bootloader has left the registers locked, writes do nothing */
+static int mt6685_rtc_verify_time(struct device *dev, struct regmap *map,
+				  struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	time64_t want = rtc_tm_to_time64(tm);
+	struct rtc_time now;
+	time64_t have;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_TRIES; i++) {
+		if (i)
+			fsleep(MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_DELAY_US);
+
+		ret = mt6685_rtc_read_tc(map, &now);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		have = rtc_tm_to_time64(&now);
+		if (have >= want && have - want <= MT6685_RTC_VERIFY_MAX_TICK)
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	/* The NTP sync of the kernel tries again every other second */
+	dev_err_ratelimited(dev,
+			    "the RTC did not take the time: wrote %ptR, reads %ptR\n",
+			    tm, &now);
+
+	return -EIO;
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_write_time(struct device *dev, struct regmap *map,
+				 struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	unsigned int bbpu;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = mt6685_rtc_write_tc(map, tm);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* The time reaches the counter with this, all at once */
+	ret = mt6685_rtc_write_reg(map, MT6685_RTC_WRTGR, 1);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout(map, MT6685_RTC_BBPU, bbpu,
+				       !(bbpu & MT6685_RTC_BBPU_CBUSY),
+				       MT6685_RTC_CBUSY_POLL_US,
+				       MT6685_RTC_CBUSY_TIMEOUT_US);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return mt6685_rtc_verify_time(dev, map, tm);
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_set_time(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
+{
+	struct regmap *map = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret, ret_gate;
+
+	ret = mt6685_rtc_ungate(map);
+	if (!ret)
+		ret = mt6685_rtc_write_time(dev, map, tm);
+
+	ret_gate = mt6685_rtc_gate(map);
+
+	return ret ?: ret_gate;
+}
+
+static const struct rtc_class_ops mt6685_rtc_ops = {
+	.read_time = mt6685_rtc_read_time,
+	.set_time = mt6685_rtc_set_time,
+};
+
+static const struct regmap_range mt6685_rtc_wr_ranges[] = {
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_SET, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN_CLR),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC, MT6685_RTC_TC_DOM + 1),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_MTH, MT6685_RTC_TC_YEA + 1),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_WRTGR, MT6685_RTC_WRTGR + 1),
+};
+
+static const struct regmap_access_table mt6685_rtc_wr_table = {
+	.yes_ranges = mt6685_rtc_wr_ranges,
+	.n_yes_ranges = ARRAY_SIZE(mt6685_rtc_wr_ranges),
+};
+
+/* Keeps debugfs away from the registers that change when read, like IRQ_STA */
+static const struct regmap_range mt6685_rtc_rd_ranges[] = {
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_HWCID, MT6685_HWCID),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN, MT6685_SCK_TOP_CKPDN),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_BBPU, MT6685_RTC_BBPU),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC, MT6685_RTC_TC_SEC),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_MIN, MT6685_RTC_TC_MIN),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_HOU, MT6685_RTC_TC_HOU),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_DOM, MT6685_RTC_TC_DOM),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_MTH, MT6685_RTC_TC_MTH),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_TC_YEA, MT6685_RTC_TC_YEA),
+	regmap_reg_range(MT6685_RTC_SPAR_MACRO, MT6685_RTC_SPAR_MACRO),
+};
+
+static const struct regmap_access_table mt6685_rtc_rd_table = {
+	.yes_ranges = mt6685_rtc_rd_ranges,
+	.n_yes_ranges = ARRAY_SIZE(mt6685_rtc_rd_ranges),
+};
+
+static const struct regmap_config mt6685_rtc_regmap_config = {
+	.reg_bits = 16,
+	.val_bits = 8,
+	.max_register = MT6685_RTC_SPAR_MACRO,
+	.wr_table = &mt6685_rtc_wr_table,
+	.rd_table = &mt6685_rtc_rd_table,
+};
+
+static void mt6685_rtc_remove_sdev(void *data)
+{
+	spmi_device_remove(data);
+}
+
+/* The regmap needs a device of its own to live on the SPMI bus */
+static struct regmap *mt6685_rtc_init_regmap(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct spmi_device *sparent = to_spmi_device(dev->parent);
+	struct spmi_device *sdev;
+	int ret;
+
+	sdev = spmi_device_alloc(sparent->ctrl);
+	if (!sdev)
+		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
+
+	sdev->usid = sparent->usid;
+	dev_set_name(&sdev->dev, "%d-%02x-rtc", sdev->ctrl->nr, sdev->usid);
+
+	ret = device_add(&sdev->dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		put_device(&sdev->dev);
+		return ERR_PTR(ret);
+	}
+
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, mt6685_rtc_remove_sdev, sdev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ERR_PTR(ret);
+
+	return devm_regmap_init_spmi_ext(sdev, &mt6685_rtc_regmap_config);
+}
+
+static int mt6685_rtc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct rtc_device *rdev;
+	struct regmap *map;
+
+	map = mt6685_rtc_init_regmap(dev);
+	if (IS_ERR(map))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(map), "cannot get regmap\n");
+
+	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, map);
+
+	rdev = devm_rtc_allocate_device(dev);
+	if (IS_ERR(rdev))
+		return PTR_ERR(rdev);
+
+	rdev->ops = &mt6685_rtc_ops;
+	rdev->range_min = mktime64(1968, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0);
+	rdev->range_max = mktime64(2095, 12, 31, 23, 59, 59);
+
+	return devm_rtc_register_device(rdev);
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id mt6685_rtc_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6685-rtc" },
+	{ /* sentinel */ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, mt6685_rtc_of_match);
+
+static struct platform_driver mt6685_rtc_driver = {
+	.driver = {
+		.name = "rtc-mt6685",
+		.of_match_table = mt6685_rtc_of_match,
+	},
+	.probe = mt6685_rtc_probe,
+};
+module_platform_driver(mt6685_rtc_driver);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("MediaTek MT6685 Clock IC RTC driver");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
